WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.409
Here's a puzzle from a textbook.

00:00:01.449 --> 00:00:02.470
Four squares in a row.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.886
Start on the left, reach the goal on
the right, and every step costs a point.

00:00:05.926 --> 00:00:07.811
Two buttons, left and right. Easy.

00:00:07.851 --> 00:00:09.640
Except in the second square,
the buttons are swapped.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.013
And you're blindfolded.

00:00:11.074 --> 00:00:14.328
Every square gives you the same reading,
and you can't keep count of your steps.

00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:18.468
So whatever you do in one square, you do
in all three. Which button do you press?

00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:21.752
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:22.202 --> 00:00:25.676
This video is about one example from
Sutton and Barto's Reinforcement Learning:

00:00:25.716 --> 00:00:29.100
An Introduction, a textbook about
machines that learn by trial and error.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.501
Here's the key to the puzzle.

00:00:30.541 --> 00:00:32.108
Any rule you could pick is really a coin:

00:00:32.128 --> 00:00:34.368
how often it presses right,
from never to always.

00:00:34.388 --> 00:00:38.308
So every answer gets a spot on this line,
and a score. We'll try them one at a time.

00:00:38.328 --> 00:00:41.131
By the end you'll know the
best answer. It isn't a button.

00:00:41.171 --> 00:00:43.760
It's a biased coin,
set to one exact number.

00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:46.341
First answer: always press right.

00:00:46.381 --> 00:00:49.212
Square one, right, you move to
square two. Square two, right.

00:00:49.252 --> 00:00:53.266
But it's swapped, so you slide back to
square one. Right. Square two. Back again.

00:00:53.306 --> 00:00:55.707
You never reach the goal.
Your score doesn't just get bad.

00:00:55.727 --> 00:00:57.138
It never stops counting.

00:00:57.942 --> 00:01:01.256
Always press left? In the first square,
left walks you into the wall.

00:01:01.296 --> 00:01:02.766
You stand there. Forever.

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:04.901
So no fixed button works.

00:01:04.941 --> 00:01:06.533
Let's give the problem
to a learning algorithm,

00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:09.999
a classic from the earlier
chapters of the book, called Sarsa.

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:12.296
It learns a score for each
button from experience.

00:01:12.336 --> 00:01:15.128
Just one score per button,
since every square reads the same.

00:01:15.168 --> 00:01:16.679
Then it mostly presses the higher one.

00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:19.675
One time in ten it presses a random
button, just to keep exploring.

00:01:19.715 --> 00:01:22.476
That's what gets it out of the
loops. I ran it thirty times.

00:01:22.516 --> 00:01:25.516
All thirty settled on the
same thing. Mostly right.

00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:28.542
And mostly right is worth about −44.

00:01:28.818 --> 00:01:30.486
The book reports the same number.

00:01:30.526 --> 00:01:35.077
The only other place this learner can
land, mostly left, is worse: about −82.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.830
Those are its two options.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.760
It's built to find a best button,
and in this corridor there isn't one.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.275
Then I tried something that felt
almost insulting. No learning at all.

00:01:44.315 --> 00:01:48.374
Flip a fair coin every step. Heads,
right. Tails, left. Minus twelve.

00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:52.015
A coin beat the learning algorithm
by more than three and a half times.

00:01:52.055 --> 00:01:54.937
And that's not a lucky run.
It's the exact value.

00:01:55.392 --> 00:01:56.672
Now, the coin isn't clever.

00:01:56.692 --> 00:01:58.212
It wins for a plain reason.

00:01:58.252 --> 00:02:00.186
The swapped square punishes commitment.

00:02:00.226 --> 00:02:03.431
Lean hard on either button and
you pay for it, bouncing or stuck.

00:02:03.471 --> 00:02:06.355
The coin doesn't commit at all.
Which raises a question.

00:02:06.395 --> 00:02:08.630
Is 50/50 actually the best you can do?

00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:12.164
So let's bias the coin.
At one end it never says right.

00:02:12.204 --> 00:02:15.748
At the other, it always does. And here's
the score for every setting in between.

00:02:15.788 --> 00:02:17.141
Both ends never finish.

00:02:17.364 --> 00:02:19.745
The learner's two options
sit way down here.

00:02:19.785 --> 00:02:22.241
The fair coin is near the
top. But it isn't the top.

00:02:22.281 --> 00:02:24.928
The best setting leans
a little toward right.

00:02:25.158 --> 00:02:27.963
The best setting works out to 2 − √2.

00:02:28.218 --> 00:02:32.266
About 58.6% right, for a score of −11.66.

00:02:32.942 --> 00:02:37.477
The book rounds it to 59% and leaves the
exact form as an exercise for the reader.

00:02:37.517 --> 00:02:39.503
And that exact form is irrational.

00:02:39.543 --> 00:02:43.960
The best way through this corridor is
a coin no one could ever mint exactly.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.087
And this is what the book
is really getting at.

00:02:46.127 --> 00:02:50.773
Almost everything before chapter 13 learns
the way Sarsa did, by scoring actions.

00:02:50.813 --> 00:02:54.574
Chapter thirteen opens with, quote,
in this chapter we consider something new.

00:02:54.614 --> 00:02:56.643
Learners that don't score buttons at all.

00:02:56.683 --> 00:02:58.498
They learn the coin's bias directly,

00:02:58.538 --> 00:03:01.479
shifting the odds toward
whatever the runs reward.

00:03:01.758 --> 00:03:03.935
The simplest one is called REINFORCE.

00:03:03.991 --> 00:03:07.499
I started it at the worst
setting I had, mostly left, −82.

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:10.814
Within 400 episodes it
was averaging about −13.

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:15.694
Across thirty runs, the average
setting it settled on was 58.6%.

00:03:15.714 --> 00:03:17.652
It found 2 − √2 on its own.

00:03:18.558 --> 00:03:19.979
The book says it plainly.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:24.782
Methods that score actions have no natural
way of finding the best random policy.

00:03:24.822 --> 00:03:26.922
Methods that learn the probabilities can.

00:03:26.962 --> 00:03:28.695
But that leaves a fair question.

00:03:28.735 --> 00:03:31.618
Why did the best answer
have to be random at all?

00:03:31.942 --> 00:03:34.169
Here's how to find out.
Take off the blindfold.

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:38.308
Same Sarsa learner as before, but now it
keeps a separate score for each square.

00:03:38.348 --> 00:03:41.142
Thirty runs out of thirty, it learned
right, left, right. Three steps.

00:03:41.162 --> 00:03:43.935
Minus three. Nearly four times
better than the best coin.

00:03:43.975 --> 00:03:45.692
And the book says that's no accident.

00:03:45.732 --> 00:03:49.565
When the player knows exactly where it is,
there's always a fixed rule that's best.

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:52.285
The coin was covering
for what it couldn't see.

00:03:52.325 --> 00:03:54.568
But randomness doesn't always disappear.

00:03:54.608 --> 00:03:55.582
The book's own example is poker,

00:03:55.622 --> 00:03:58.911
where the best play is often to
bluff with a specific probability.

00:03:58.951 --> 00:04:00.188
Here's a simpler one I ran.

00:04:00.228 --> 00:04:02.965
Rock, paper, scissors, against an
opponent that learns your habits.

00:04:02.985 --> 00:04:05.975
Nothing is hidden. Always rock
lost essentially every round.

00:04:06.015 --> 00:04:07.863
Leaning toward rock still came out behind.

00:04:07.903 --> 00:04:09.953
The even three-way split broke even.

00:04:09.993 --> 00:04:12.165
Now swap in an opponent
that just plays at random,

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.597
and always rock does as well as anything.

00:04:14.864 --> 00:04:18.805
That randomness is there because
someone is watching for a pattern.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.016
So that's the test, and you
can carry it to other problems.

00:04:22.056 --> 00:04:25.017
When the best move is random,
give the player more information.

00:04:25.057 --> 00:04:28.645
If the randomness disappears, it was
covering for what the player couldn't see.

00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:31.287
If it survives, look for
someone who'd exploit a pattern.

00:04:31.327 --> 00:04:32.944
The corridor is the first kind.

00:04:32.984 --> 00:04:35.102
Rock, paper, scissors is the second.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:39.699
Now try it on a goalkeeper facing a
penalty, choosing which way to dive.

00:04:39.739 --> 00:04:40.452
Run the test.

00:04:40.492 --> 00:04:43.583
Does more information make the
randomness disappear, or does it survive?

00:04:43.623 --> 00:04:45.429
Put your answer in the comments.

00:04:45.469 --> 00:04:47.010
A strong answer names
the missing information,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.088
or the person who'd exploit the pattern.

00:04:49.128 --> 00:04:51.467
Saying it's random because
it's uncertain doesn't count.

00:04:51.507 --> 00:04:54.073
And if you code, the corridor
script is linked below.

00:04:54.113 --> 00:04:56.525
Run it, and watch the blindfold come off.

00:04:56.975 --> 00:04:58.183
Two minus root two.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:01.741
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for
Humanitarians AI, signing off.
